WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:03.830
Every data engineering loop has a SQL round.

2
00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:06.280
Senior candidates do not fail on syntax.

3
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.365
They fail on edge cases.

4
00:00:08.385 --> 00:00:14.415
Three skills cover most rounds: window functions, deduplication, and reading a plan.

5
00:00:14.435 --> 00:00:16.475
First: window functions.

6
00:00:16.495 --> 00:00:20.000
A window function looks at other rows, but keeps every row.

7
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.010
Group by would collapse them.

8
00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:24.023
Row number gives one, two, three.

9
00:00:24.043 --> 00:00:25.923
Rank leaves gaps after a tie.

10
00:00:25.943 --> 00:00:28.145
Dense rank leaves no gaps.

11
00:00:28.165 --> 00:00:29.507
The trap is ties.

12
00:00:29.527 --> 00:00:33.545
Always say what happens when two rows have the same value.

13
00:00:33.565 --> 00:00:35.805
Second: keep one row per key.

14
00:00:35.825 --> 00:00:38.618
Number the rows inside each key, newest first.

15
00:00:38.638 --> 00:00:40.615
Then keep row number one.

16
00:00:40.635 --> 00:00:45.375
But if two rows share the same time, the result can change on every run.

17
00:00:45.395 --> 00:00:47.287
So add a tie-breaker to the order.

18
00:00:47.307 --> 00:00:50.250
Now the query is deterministic.

19
00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:52.385
Third: streaks and sessions.

20
00:00:52.405 --> 00:00:54.798
A user logs in five days in a row.

21
00:00:54.818 --> 00:00:56.645
How do you find that streak?

22
00:00:56.665 --> 00:00:58.683
Subtract the row number from the date.

23
00:00:58.703 --> 00:01:02.070
Rows in one streak get the same result.

24
00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:04.380
Last: why is the query slow?

25
00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:05.230
Do not guess.

26
00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:06.217
Read the plan.

27
00:01:06.237 --> 00:01:08.740
Find the step that takes the most time.

28
00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:10.603
Filter on the partition column.

29
00:01:10.623 --> 00:01:12.140
Select fewer columns.

30
00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:15.565
And watch for joins that multiply rows.

31
00:01:15.585 --> 00:01:18.275
Quick recap of SQL for senior loops.

32
00:01:18.295 --> 00:01:19.450
Windows keep rows.

33
00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:20.875
Dedupe with a tie-breaker.

34
00:01:20.895 --> 00:01:23.185
And read the plan before you tune.

35
00:01:23.205 --> 00:01:26.305
Now start the first lesson below.
